# dark-gray - Qt/KDE colour OVERRIDES.
#
# Empty on purpose. apply.sh DERIVES the KDE colour roles from palette.css via
# lib/derive.py, so colours live in exactly one place and cannot drift between
# the GTK and Qt sides. This file is only for KDE roles that have no palette
# equivalent at all; it is applied after the derived values, so anything here
# wins.
#
# DecorationFocus/DecorationHover used to be written here as a literal
# 110,118,129. That was a second copy of the accent, and it stopped matching
# the moment palette.css changed - so they are derived now too, from
# accent_bg_color. Do not put them back.
#
# ── Why the Qt side needs kdeglobals at all ───────────────────────────────────
#
# Qt cannot read CSS, so palette.css never reaches it directly. But you also do
# not hand-write the Qt palette here, because Kvantum supplies it and wins:
# measured, setting Colors:Window to magenta left the Qt palette completely
# unchanged at #101010. That is why apply.sh now also generates a Kvantum theme
# from palette.css - see the Kvantum section there. THAT is what recolours
# Dolphin.
#
# What kdeglobals still decides on its own is QStyleHints::colorScheme(). With
# a LIGHT kdeglobals and a DARK Kvantum theme the result was:
#
#     palette #101010 (dark, Kvantum)  +  colorScheme Light (kdeglobals)
#
# KF6 apps consult both, which is how Dolphin ended up drawing white rows on a
# dark palette. So kdeglobals and the Kvantum theme must agree on dark-vs-light.
# Deriving both from palette.css is what guarantees that.
#
# These values also become the real palette if Kvantum is ever not the style -
# with QT_STYLE_OVERRIDE=Fusion the same light kdeglobals produced a genuinely
# light palette (#fcfcfc).
#
# ── Format, if you do need an override ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# [Colors:Window]
# SomeRoleWithNoPaletteEquivalent=110,118,129
